{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/20780-grammatical-idiosyncrasies","id":20780,"title":"Grammatical Idiosyncrasies","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-30T14:00:12-08:00","id":20780,"title":"Grammatical Idiosyncrasies","content":"I consider myself to be pretty decent at speaking and writing the English language, the only one I know. I'm fond of using dashes, colons, and even <i>semi</i>-colons when I write--even if it's just in a text--to properly convey the cadence I'm feeling in my speech. I'm not above screwing things up, mind you, and I certainly don't know all the rules, but my hackles are raised when I see someone confusing homophones.<br /> <br /> But there are two things I do that are technically incorrect by our current standards of grammar in the US because dammit, I am <i>right</i> about them!<br /> <br /> The first is &quot;andor&quot;. Normally when you combine the conjunctions &quot;and&quot; and &quot;or&quot; you put a slash between them (&quot;and/or&quot;), but I find slashes inelegant and excessive when a simple compound word would fit more of our standard rules! Sure, people really emphasize and &quot;pronounce&quot; that slash, but I think that's an artifact of the punctuation as opposed to naturally evolving spoken language patterns.<br /> <br /> The second is how I deal with end punctuation when quotes are involved. In the US at least, you're always supposed to put punctuation marks <i>within</i> the quote marks, regardless of whether or not the punctuation is part of the quote. This just smacks of misattributing to me, since you're adding or changing emphasis just by those lines and dots being there, so I always keep them on the outside unless the quote I'm using also contains the same punctuation. I've done some research on this, and some sources say that the practice of quote marks including other punctuation comes from typeface practices where the punctuation mark pieces where tiny and fragile, and enclosing them inside the quote marks helped protect them from damage. Apocryphal? Probably, but it's the only explanation for this I've found that's reasonable.<br /> <br /> Am I a madman? Does anyone else do these things? Or do you have other grammar things of your own?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Cxuog44JenzbN7GaAyRqDO4DXce9VGv6ybaCv9yYsPk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Simulacre: An Alternative Morality Stat for Prometheans</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/9523-the-alchemy-lab-homebrew-athanors\" target=\"_blank\">Homebrew Athanors</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/887-metamurpho","name":"Metamurpho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar887_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"228"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T15:21:07-08:00","id":20818,"title":"","content":"For casual conversations, I kind of just write for simplicity-I very rarely worry about being grammatically correct when I speak to y'all, for example.<br /> <br /> Professionally, I clean up a lot more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T16:12:53-08:00","id":20844,"title":"","content":"Isn't &quot;or&quot; inclusive? In spanish you use just &quot;or&quot; when you want to imply and/or. In fact using and/or is frowned upon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T17:34:50-08:00","id":20880,"title":"","content":"The slash in <i>and/or</i> might just be the exact opposite of &quot;inelegant and excessive.&quot; It conveys the meaning of XOR with a single stroke, and there is basically no ambiguity in this. Conversely, your suggested compound form does not register as such to me, appearing instead to have an entirely different etymology and meaning,<br /> <br /> You'll note that I placed that period inside the quotation; just a year ago, I would not have done that. Now, however, I have come to find the alternative aesthetically unsatisfying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/97-the-mg","name":"The MG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar97_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T22:54:47-08:00","id":21010,"title":"","content":"Technically, the titles of books and movies and whatnot are supposed to be italicized. I don't do that, because I think italics are very much overused, particularly on the internet. I bold titles instead, since bolding seems to be almost completely useless otherwise; you can't even bold words for emphasis because it comes off a bit shout-y.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-01T05:10:40-08:00","id":21119,"title":"","content":"are these even grammatical in nature seriously<br /> <br /> (This one isn't, having instead to do with terminology.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/97-the-mg","name":"The MG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar97_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-01T09:14:00-08:00","id":21177,"title":"","content":"People like to treat Formal English like it's the One True English, but it's not. Formal English is a dialect, like any other dialect, and its rules evolve like any other dialect, separately from the others. People also like to pretend that formal english is the only <i>appropriate</i> dialect for any situation and if you're using any other you're &quot;wrong&quot;. Which is equally silly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-01T12:18:40-08:00","id":21245,"title":"","content":"Myself, I tend put periods outside the quote. If there's a period in the quote as well, I sometime put both, if that punctuation is important for the context or the point I'm trying to make. Example: Markus said, &quot;My house is on fire.&quot;.<br /> <br /> I, like the OP, don't use the / symbol if I can at all help it. However, in the case of &quot;and/or&quot; I make an exception. The reason is that the / means or by itself, and so I can't use or there, or it'd be strange. Compounding them into one word, &quot;andor&quot; is a solution which seems reasonable to me, though. If y'all Americans want to do that, you have my Swedish approval.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Dex Davican wrote:</b> I can say without exaggeration or dishonesty that I am the most creative man <i>ever to have lived</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/75-weimann","name":"Weimann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar75_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1279"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T07:41:11-08:00","id":21615,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The MG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/20780-grammatical-idiosyncrasies?p=20880#post20880\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The slash in <i>and/or</i> might just be the exact opposite of &quot;inelegant and excessive.&quot; It conveys the meaning of XOR with a single stroke, and there is basically no ambiguity in this. Conversely, your suggested compound form does not register as such to me, appearing instead to have an entirely different etymology and meaning,<br /> <br /> You'll note that I placed that period inside the quotation; just a year ago, I would not have done that. Now, however, I have come to find the alternative aesthetically unsatisfying.</div> </div> </div> </div>and/or does not mean XOR. It means inclusive or. &quot;and/or&quot; means &quot;and or or&quot;, hence the slash.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T07:44:32-08:00","id":21617,"title":"","content":"I was referring to the slash, not the totality of the term.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/97-the-mg","name":"The MG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar97_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-06T13:33:11-08:00","id":24602,"title":"","content":"On the topic of placement of the punctuation, I tried writing a novel with the punctuation outside the quotations. The aesthetics alone convinced me that it belongs on the inside. In addition, since the punctuation should indicate the emphasis intended within the quote, it makes more sense to me to place it inside. If quoting out of context or taking an incomplete portion, that's where the trailing periods come in.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}